  "content": {
    "text": "BREAKING NEWS\nOur analysts just identified a stock with the potential to be the next Nvidia. Tell us how you invest and we'll show you why it's our #1 pick. Tap here.\nThe Trump Accounts app launched on Thursday, part of the Administration’s promise to help America’s children save for the future.\nBut the biggest winner here, besides American babies, is Robinhood, which will power the app and be the first way millions of young Americans interact with the stock market. And never one to shrug off any credit, Trump, of course, named the funds after himself.\nThe app, in short, allows parents to manage investment accounts for their kids. As part of the Big Beautiful Bill Trump signed into law on July 4 last year, the federal government will seed eligible accounts with $1,000 that will be invested in the stock market. Parents will be the sole custodians of the account until the child turns 18, and will be able to start making contributions by July 4, on the country’s 250th birthday.\nAnyone born between January 1, 2025 and December 31, 2028 is eligible for the account, and any child under 18 is able to open one on the app but won’t receive the federal money. In order to open the account, parents need to download the app from the app store and sign IRS Form 4547 to create the account and request the $1,000. Similar to an IRA, the money can’t be withdrawn until the child turns 18.\n“For the first time ever, we're going to give every newborn American child a financial stake in the future,” Trump said at an event earlier this year about the accounts. “Over the next 15 years, we're going to put $3 to $4 trillion of wealth into the hands of young Americans who otherwise would have really started out with nothing.”\nIf you need a remedial math lesson, the numbers compound quickly. If you assume the market returns an average of 10% each year, $1,000 would return about $5,700. By retirement age, the account will be well over $200,000. If you contribute $1,000 each year to the account, it would be worth over $55,000 by year 18. If you contribute $5,000, it would be worth well over $250,000 by the time Junior figures out how to put a fitted sheet on the bed in their freshman-year dorm room.\nOne stock. Nvidia-level potential. 30M+ investors trust Moby to find it first. Get the pick. Tap here.\nPoking around the slickly designed app isn’t much different from the interfaces of venture-backed fintech startups telling users to “take control” of their financial future. Which makes sense, considering Robinhood helped build it alongside BNY.",
    "text_blocks": [
      "BREAKING NEWS",
      "Our analysts just identified a stock with the potential to be the next Nvidia. Tell us how you invest and we'll show you why it's our #1 pick. Tap here.",
      "The Trump Accounts app launched on Thursday, part of the Administration’s promise to help America’s children save for the future.",
      "But the biggest winner here, besides American babies, is Robinhood, which will power the app and be the first way millions of young Americans interact with the stock market. And never one to shrug off any credit, Trump, of course, named the funds after himself.",
      "The app, in short, allows parents to manage investment accounts for their kids. As part of the Big Beautiful Bill Trump signed into law on July 4 last year, the federal government will seed eligible accounts with $1,000 that will be invested in the stock market. Parents will be the sole custodians of the account until the child turns 18, and will be able to start making contributions by July 4, on the country’s 250th birthday.",
      "Anyone born between January 1, 2025 and December 31, 2028 is eligible for the account, and any child under 18 is able to open one on the app but won’t receive the federal money. In order to open the account, parents need to download the app from the app store and sign IRS Form 4547 to create the account and request the $1,000. Similar to an IRA, the money can’t be withdrawn until the child turns 18.",
      "“For the first time ever, we're going to give every newborn American child a financial stake in the future,” Trump said at an event earlier this year about the accounts. “Over the next 15 years, we're going to put $3 to $4 trillion of wealth into the hands of young Americans who otherwise would have really started out with nothing.”",
      "If you need a remedial math lesson, the numbers compound quickly. If you assume the market returns an average of 10% each year, $1,000 would return about $5,700. By retirement age, the account will be well over $200,000. If you contribute $1,000 each year to the account, it would be worth over $55,000 by year 18. If you contribute $5,000, it would be worth well over $250,000 by the time Junior figures out how to put a fitted sheet on the bed in their freshman-year dorm room.",
      "One stock. Nvidia-level potential. 30M+ investors trust Moby to find it first. Get the pick. Tap here.",
      "Poking around the slickly designed app isn’t much different from the interfaces of venture-backed fintech startups telling users to “take control” of their financial future. Which makes sense, considering Robinhood helped build it alongside BNY."
    ],
